<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  A live 1 cm reconstruction is a genuinely large-memory app, and the default
  per-app jetsam limit is what killed it: at 3.2 M triangles the mesh arenas held
  3089 MB (resident_arena_bytes, summed over the mesh_slots ring) beside an
  805 MB voxel grid, and the OS sent SIGKILL -- no OutOfMemory to report, the
  process simply disappears.

  increased-memory-limit raises that jetsam ceiling on devices that have the RAM
  (this iPad Pro reports 10922 MB).

  extended-virtual-addressing would normally sit beside it -- the arena ring can
  exceed the 4 GB a 32-bit address space covers -- but it is deliberately absent:
  a **personal** (free) development team cannot provision it. Xcode fails the
  build outright with "Personal development teams ... do not support the Extended
  Virtual Addressing capability", so adding it back costs the whole build, not
  just the capability. It needs a paid Apple Developer Program membership with
  the capability enabled on the App ID. increased-memory-limit alone does
  provision on a personal team, and is verifiable in the signed binary with
  `codesign -d --entitlements -`.

  Not a licence to be careless: this moves the wall, it does not remove it.
  voxel_size, FusionConfig::max_buckets and mesh_slots are still what decide
  whether a scan fits, and the occupancy guard in Fusion::fuse is still what
  keeps the *GPU* out of trouble independently of any of this.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
